  Each chapter was read in full and every displayed step was rechecked
  (algebra, signs, quantifiers, domains, limits). "No defect" below means no
  substantive mathematical defect was found in this pass; it is author-side
  evidence, not a review.

  <section|Repaired gap (substantive)><label|repaired-gap-substantive>

  <strong|F1. The Poincaré-invariance hypothesis of the word construction was
  never discharged.> Proposition <verbatim|prop:word-motion>
  (reconstruction.tex) assumes <verbatim|(eq:word-motion-input)>:
  K(gb,gc)=K(b,c) and continuity of g\<mapsto\>K(b,gc). The chapter said
  these were "to be established before this construction is applied", and no
  chapter in the 873-page draft established them (checked by grepping all
  <verbatim|\\ref>s to the equation, the proposition and the input). The
  endpoint Definition <verbatim|ex-reconstructed> needs the resulting
  strongly continuous unitary Poincaré representation, so this was a real
  hole in the dependency chain, not only a presentational one.

  The pinned source proves exactly this statement as
  <verbatim|YM.Work.C28.answer> (<verbatim|Lean/YM/Work/C28.lean>; consumed
  through <verbatim|YM.Integrated.reconstructionArcs.c\<nu\>> in
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Next/BOUNDARY/Stage.lean> and by
  <verbatim|YM.Boundary.harvest> via <verbatim|Surface.Strong>/<verbatim|Surface.Transforms>
  in <verbatim|Lean/YM/Fields/Lo.lean>). <verbatim|reconstruction-evidence.md>
  lists C01\UC27 and C29 but not C28, which explains the omission.

  Repair: new Corollary <verbatim|cor:word-motion-inputs> in
  <verbatim|boundary-detection.tex>, with a detailed proof following C28's
  three steps: (i) the star commutes with the motion (<verbatim|rev_po>,
  using <verbatim|J \<rho\> = \<rho\> J> from
  <verbatim|prop:lorentz-label-descent>); (ii) invariance of the word
  functional, splitting g=(a,I)(0,\<Lambda\>) and applying translation
  invariance then Lorentz covariance from <verbatim|thm:lorentz-covariance>
  (<verbatim|expect_po>, <verbatim|gpush_split>); (iii) continuity by pinning
  all labels in one finite level, expanding in a basis with continuous
  coefficients, and using Schwartz continuity of the affine pullback
  (<verbatim|strong>, <verbatim|continuous_pinned>,
  <verbatim|continuous_push>). It also records that the translation
  hypothesis of <verbatim|prop:word-motion> is <verbatim|prop:heat-cups>. The
  corollary is placed after <verbatim|cor:word-test-inputs>, which discharges
  the other inputs of the same construction.

  <strong|F2. Same-data requirement on the involution J (new edge
  C03-C14-to-C04-involution-identity).> The word construction (heat-vectors,
  reconstruction) uses the reflected conjugate J of
  <verbatim|reflection.tex>; <verbatim|prop:lorentz-label-descent> proves
  commutation with the involution J_d of <verbatim|input:calibrated-actions>.
  F1 needs these to be the same map. The text after the corollary shows this
  is forced, not conventional: with \<theta\> the time reflection and
  \<kappa\> the real structure, \<theta\>\<kappa\> commutes with K_a=-iL_a,
  while \<kappa\> alone anticommutes. In Lean the identification is the
  hypothesis <verbatim|YM.Boundary.starRead J h> (J acts as the time-axis
  adjoint of the real spin) used through <verbatim|YM.Boundary.Letter.rewrites>.
  C14 states that its Q_d preserve "reality and signed motions", which would
  give J_dQ_d=Q_dJ_d^0 for J=\<theta\>\<kappa\>, but the C14 author should
  confirm this explicitly.

  <section|Notation repairs (no mathematical
  change)><label|notation-repairs-no-mathematical-change>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item><strong|N1.> The letter V denoted both the unitary time group of
    <verbatim|prop:complex-heat> and the isometry H\<rightarrow\>H_E of
    <verbatim|prop:word-motion>, and the two met in the proof of
    <verbatim|thm:field-spectrum>. The isometry is now \<cal-V\>
    (<verbatim|\\mathcal V>) in reconstruction.tex, spectral-measure.tex and
    complex-heat.tex. The apologetic sentence in complex-heat.tex is removed.

    <item><strong|N2.> reconstruction.tex wrote U_E(a) for the joint
    Euclidean translation group, which complex-heat.tex and
    spectral-measure.tex call T_E (U_E there is spatial only). It now uses
    T_E, introduced in the statement.

    <item><strong|N3.> In <verbatim|prop:word-motion>, "h_t(ab)" used a for a
    translation acting on a word, while the same paragraph used ab for
    concatenation. It now reads h_t(\<tau\>_a b).
  </itemize-minus>

