  <section|Observations for other packages (no edit
  made)><label|observations-for-other-packages-no-edit-made>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item><strong|O1 (for G03, bibliography).> complex-heat.tex cites
    <verbatim|\\cite[Theorem 2.19]{Williams}> and
    <verbatim|reconstruction-evidence.md> says "Theorem 2.19, printed p.34",
    but the bibitem in <verbatim|existence.tex> says "Theorem 2.13, pp.
    29--31". One of them is wrong. The Dartmouth URL was blocked from this
    sandbox (proxy 403), so I could not check which.

    <item><strong|O2 (for G06, dependency closure).>
    <verbatim|lem:zero-boundary> follows the source route through the full
    tube, so <verbatim|prop:boundary-detection> inherits the
    Lorentz-covariance hypothesis through <verbatim|cor:cone-support>.
    Mathematically the same conclusion also follows from
    <verbatim|lem:exponential-testing> alone: if W_n(v)=0 then \<psi\>_y
    vanishes on Fourier transforms of all compactly supported tests, a dense
    subspace, so F_n(<ym-middot>+iy)=0. I did not change the chapter because
    the maintainer asked for main-step fidelity and this exact shortcut was
    removed earlier (see <verbatim|reviews/draft-review.md>). G06 may use the
    observation to confirm that label detection does not depend on the
    calibrated Lorentz input.

    <item><strong|O3 (for G01).> <verbatim|thm:field-spectrum>'s uniqueness
    clause needs the heat-fixed space to be C\<Omega\>_E. The Lean variant
    <verbatim|YM.Next.BOUNDARY.Stage.establishing> omits that hypothesis and
    its conclusion; <verbatim|YM.Boundary.harvest> keeps it
    (<verbatim|C.UniqueHeat>). G01 should check which one the public endpoint
    uses.
  </itemize-minus>

